                             Lynx README file

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIS FORK: OpenVMS VAX -- QUICK INSTALL
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This tree is Lynx 2.9.3 with the fixes needed to build and run on OpenVMS VAX
V7.3 with DEC C.  Most importantly it fixes a bug that makes stock 2.9.3 crash
on every http: URL when built with DEC C (see kit/RELEASE_NOTES.TXT).

If you just want to install it, you do NOT need to build anything.  A prebuilt
VMSINSTAL kit is in the kit/ directory.

  1. Copy kit/LYNX293.A to your VAX.  Transfer it in BINARY / IMAGE mode --
     it is a BACKUP save set and ASCII mode will corrupt it silently.

  2. On the VAX, repair the record attributes.  A file that has travelled by
     FTP or inside a zip arrives as a stream file, and BACKUP will not read it:

         $ SET FILE/ATTRIBUTES=(RFM:FIX,MRS:2048,LRL:2048,RAT:NONE) LYNX293.A

     Check with DIRECTORY/FULL -- it must say "Fixed length 2048 byte records"
     and 4540 blocks used (the allocated figure after the slash depends on
     the disk cluster size and may differ).

  3. Install it:

         $ @SYS$UPDATE:VMSINSTAL LYNX293 device:[directory-holding-the-saveset]

     Answer YES to "processes are still active" (those are the TCP/IP
     services; the install does not touch them) and YES to the backup
     question.

  4. Turn on the command, and use it:

         $ @SYS$MANAGER:LYNX$STARTUP
         $ LYNX -version
         $ LYNX http://some.host/

  5. Make it work for EVERY user, not just the account you installed from.
     Add the same line to BOTH of these files -- they do different jobs and
     neither one substitutes for the other:

         SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM    $ @SYS$MANAGER:LYNX$STARTUP
             runs at boot with privilege, and defines the Lynx_Dir logical
             /SYSTEM so every process can see it.  Without Lynx_Dir, Lynx
             exits immediately with "Configuration file is not available".

         SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM          $ @SYS$MANAGER:LYNX$STARTUP
             runs at every login, and defines the LYNX command.  A DCL
             symbol is per-process and CANNOT be made system-wide, so every
             user's login has to define it.

     Installed and verified on a real MicroVAX running OpenVMS VAX V7.3: an
     account holding only NETMBX and TMPMBX renders pages correctly.  If you
     installed from a kit built before 29-AUG-2026, see kit/README.md,
     "Already installed from an older kit?" -- those kits defined Lynx_Dir
     as a process logical and worked for one account only.

     A VMS wrinkle worth knowing when you make those two edits: SYS$MANAGER
     is a search list (SYS$SPECIFIC first, then SYS$COMMON).  An editor that
     writes a NEW file rather than a new version will drop it in
     SYS$SPECIFIC:[SYSMGR], where it silently shadows the real one in
     SYS$COMMON:[SYSMGR].  Edit SYS$COMMON:[SYSMGR]... explicitly, and check
     with  DIRECTORY SYS$SPECIFIC:[SYSMGR]SYLOGIN.COM  that you did not
     leave a stray copy behind.

The browser lands in SYS$SYSTEM:, its configuration in SYS$COMMON:[LYNX]LYNX.CFG.

BOOKMARKS

Every user gets their own bookmark file, SYS$LOGIN:lynx_bookmarks.html, with
no setup at all, and their own bookmarks are their start page.  Press "a" to
add a bookmark, "v" to view them.  "LYNX <url>" still works normally.

Two traps, both documented in kit/README.md:

  * "BOOKMARK_FILE" is NOT a lynx.cfg keyword -- only DEFAULT_BOOKMARK_FILE
    is -- and it must stay a BARE FILENAME, because Lynx prepends the home
    directory itself.  An absolute path gives everyone one shared file.

  * "Can't access startfile" is usually a FILE PROTECTION problem, not a URL
    syntax problem.  A start page everyone must read has to be World:RE.

No SSL.  https: URLs will not work; use an HTTP-to-HTTPS proxy on another
machine and define the "https_proxy" logical.

Full details, and how to rebuild the kit from source, are in kit/README.md.
Build-from-source instructions are in INSTALLATION section III and in
VAX_INSTALL_README.md.

